# converted from 02-the-fifth-year.md
@title The Fifth Year
@bg ashford
@mood grief

narrate He was twenty-four, and he had been in the Order five years, and he had a reputation he did not want.
narrate They called him the Ashford Boy. Not to his face — nobody called him anything to his face, because he had a way of going very quiet when people were kind to him, and men found that unsettling in an officer. But they called him it, and it had got around, and by the fifth year it had stopped being a joke.
narrate He had opened the line four more times.
narrate That was the thing. The Warden had promised a thousand a year and had delivered a thousand a year for two years, and then Corin had gone to him with a map and an argument, and the third year it had been two thousand, and the fifth year six.
narrate Forty thousand people in the Mire. At six thousand a year, the arithmetic said seven years.
narrate Corin did the arithmetic every night. That was the other thing they said about him.
narrate He was losing things.
narrate That was the price, and he had known it going in, and knowing it going in is not the same as living in it.
narrate It did not come all at once. That was the design, and it was a good design, because it meant you never noticed. It came the way a river takes a bank: a little, every day, until one morning you look up and the field is somewhere else.
narrate The first thing he lost was the smell of Maeve's cottage.
narrate He noticed on the fourth month. He was walking past a herb garden in Ashford and he stopped, because a woman was growing something that should have been familiar, and there was nothing there — a smooth, quiet space where the smell of a cottage in the Mire used to be, the way a room looks after someone has cleaned it.
narrate He stood in the street for a while.
narrate Then he went to the barracks and wrote it down.
narrate He kept a ledger now.
narrate That was Bryn's idea — Bryn, who had come to Ashford in the second year and had never once explained why, and had simply started living in the eastern barracks and drinking with the men and being useful, the way he had always been useful.
narrate So Corin kept a ledger, and in it he wrote, every night:
narrate That was the shape of his life. He wrote it all down, and every night he read back four pages, to check for holes.
narrate The holes were the worst of it.
narrate Not the forgetting itself. The holes. Because the mark did not take a thing cleanly; it took a thing and left the shape of it, so that you could be sitting in a barracks reading your own handwriting about a woman who gave you bread at the side of a road and have no memory of the road.
narrate There were eleven such entries by the fifth year. Eleven things he had written down about his own life and could not feel.
narrate And the eleventh was the worst, and it was in the fifth year, and it was this:
narrate He read that entry four times.
narrate Then he went and found Bryn, who was drinking in the hall, and sat down opposite him, and said:
corin neutral Tell me about the winter in the Hollow.
narrate Bryn looked at him for a long moment.
corin neutral There wasn't one,
bryn neutral That's what I thought.
corin neutral There was never a winter in the Hollow. I was—
corin neutral I was testing you. To see if you'd catch it.
bryn neutral And I caught it.
corin neutral You wrote it down and you checked.
corin neutral That's the system working. That's what the ledger is for.
bryn neutral That's not what I asked.
corin neutral I know.
narrate Corin waited.
narrate And Bryn — who had spent eleven years being unable to say things — put down his cup, and looked at a young man he had watched for five years, and said:
bryn neutral There was never a winter in the Hollow. There was a spring, and it was the spring after you were born, and I was there, and your mother was there, and your father was there. And you were four months old and you had a cough and your mother would not sleep, and I sat up with her for three nights, and that is the only time in my life I have ever been any use to anybody.
narrate Corin did not move.
corin neutral Why did you tell me it was the Hollow.
bryn neutral Because the Hollow was where you almost died,
bryn neutral and I wanted to know whether you still had it.
corin neutral And I don't.
bryn neutral You wrote it down,
bryn neutral That's the same thing.
corin neutral It is not the same thing.
bryn neutral No,
bryn neutral It isn't.
narrate Corin went up onto the wall.
narrate He did that most nights. It had become the only place he could think, because the barracks had people in it and the people wanted things from him, and the wall had the east and nothing else.
narrate He looked east at the Mire, and he did the arithmetic — six thousand a year, seven years to go — and he put his marked hand on the stone and looked at the black circle in his palm.
corin neutral You're taking the wrong things,
narrate The mark did not answer. It never answered. It had not answered since a field eleven miles north of a village that no longer existed, and he had stopped expecting it to, and he said it anyway, every night, because the alternative was to stop saying things to it, and he was afraid of what he would become if he did.
corin neutral You're taking the wrong things,
corin neutral Take the Fever. Take the war. Take anything. Leave me the small ones.
narrate Nothing.
narrate He stood there until the watch changed.
